'' A WIDE LITERAL IS EMITTED AS CODE UNITS, not as its source bytes.
''
'' EmitGlobal tested 'isString' before 'isWide' and returned -- and a wide literal is
'' BOTH, because AddWideStringUnits sets the two flags together so that the merge
'' lists and the dump can ask "is this a string" before asking how wide it is. The
'' wide branch, and WideArray with it, was therefore dead code from the day it was
'' written: every wstring literal reached the linker as an [N x i8] of its SOURCE
'' TEXT, and the runtime read those bytes as uint16 code units.
''
'' Two ASCII characters became one CJK one. Measured, before the fix:
''
''     dim as string k = wstr( "fOoBaR" )     printed  ????
''     ww.a = "wid"                           read past the end of a 4-byte global
''
'' and the MIR was correct in both cases -- 'fb_WstrAssignToA_Init(@k, -1, @@$wstr0,
'' 0)' is exactly fbc's call -- which is why the two 'lowerwstr.bas' entry points the
'' bug was first attributed to were both innocent. A --dump-ir test could not have
'' caught this: the dump prints the wcharPool, which was right all along.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --emit-ir tests/codegen/src/wide-literal.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0

'' i16 ON WINDOWS -- the width is the TARGET's, which is why MIR keeps units and not
'' bytes. The trailing 0 is the terminator and is not part of the count.
'' EXPECT-OUT: @"$wstr0" = private unnamed_addr constant [7 x i16] [i16 102, i16 79, i16 111, i16 66, i16 97, i16 82, i16 0], align 2

'' THE NARROW LITERAL BESIDE IT still goes out as bytes: the reorder must not have
'' captured the ordinary case.
'' EXPECT-OUT: private unnamed_addr constant [2 x i8] c"[\00", align 1

'' A UNIT ABOVE 255 survives, which is the half of the bug a same-width ASCII literal
'' cannot show: one byte per unit would have truncated 0x4E2D to 0x2D.
'' EXPECT-OUT: [i16 233, i16 20013, i16 0], align 2

'' AND NO WIDE LITERAL IS EMITTED AS BYTES. 'fOoBaR' as an [N x i8] is exactly what
'' the bug produced, and it is the one spelling that must never appear again.
'' EXPECT-NOT: c"fOoBaR\00"
'' EXPECT-NOT: c"wid\00"
